sara (Acme): Hey, hitting a production crash. Our client called `json_mode=true` and the API returned malformed JSON repeatedly, causing our parser to blow up. request_id=req_0b9f, model=redwood-infer-7b, temp=0.2, streaming=false. Sample raw response below — missing commas/quotes. Can you advise?

maya (support): thanks for the heads up @sara — can you paste the exact raw response you saw and confirm if you're using any client-side postprocessing? also roughly how often does this occur (pct of calls)?

sara (Acme): Raw response (copied from logs):
```
{"status": "ok","items": [{"id": 1 "name": "alpha"}{"id": 2, "name": "beta"}], "meta": {"count":2}}
```
We get this ~3-5% of calls. No client postprocessing besides JSON.parse.

maya (support): ok reproduced pattern. quick mitigation: (1) client-side lenient sanitizer, (2) retry-with-stricter-formatting, (3) if still broken, ask model to "repair" the output. sharing some snippets.

jon (eng): fyi heuristics can work but be careful about silently changing semantics. recommended retry policy: up to 3 total attempts with exponential backoff + jitter. e.g. delays: 500ms, 1s, 2s (add small rand up to 200ms). Lower temperature and narrow the output schema on retries. Also record raw broken outputs for offline eval.

li (ml): for the "repair" step you can send a dedicated correction prompt. Example: `You were asked to return JSON only. The model output is below (may be malformed). Please return only corrected valid JSON that matches the schema: {items:[{id:number,name:string}],meta:{count:number}}. If you cannot confidently correct, return {items:[],meta:{count:0}}.` Always ask for "JSON only" and set temperature=0.

maya (support): quick client-side JS sanitizer we use for similar cases (best-effort, non-destructive):
```js
function quickSanitize(raw){
  // insert comma between `}{` -> `},{`
  raw = raw.replace(/}\s*{/g, '},{');
  // close unquoted keys (very conservative)
  raw = raw.replace(/([\{,])\s*([a-zA-Z0-9_]+)\s*:/g,'$1"$2":');
  // remove trailing commas before ] or }
  raw = raw.replace(/,\s*(]|})/g,'$1');
  // try parse, else return null
  try{ return JSON.parse(raw);}catch(e){return null;}
}
```

jon (eng): +1. Add a logging wrapper that stores both the raw and sanitized outputs and a flag if sanitizer changed the result. For service-side fixes, we can add a server option to run a repair prompt automatically when `json_mode=true` — pinging infra for feasibility.

li (ml): alternative approach: instead of sanitizing, do a post-call repair roundtrip: send the raw string to the model with instruction: `Return ONLY the corrected JSON.` This often preserves intent and fixes subtle quoting issues. Works well when failures are small. We should also include the expected schema to avoid hallucinated fields.

maya (support): put together a recovery flow for your client: 1) try parse; 2) if parse fails -> run quickSanitize(); 3) if still fails -> retry call with temp=0, max_tokens constrained and explicit "JSON only" instruction; 4) if retry returns malformed -> send repair prompt (provide raw output to model); 5) if still fails -> surface error to user and store example for ML team.

support-bot: Created ticket INC-2026 and assigned to eng oncall. :rotating_light:

jon (eng): also collect ~10 examples (raw) and drop them in the reproducer folder. we'll add a small test harness to track regression rate. For production, I'd recommend a per-route feature flag `strict_json_mode` that enforces server-side re-ask+repair so clients can opt in.

sara (Acme): thank you — we'll implement the sanitize + retry + repair flow. one question: recommended retry count? and should we add backoff client-side or rely on server?

maya (support): recommended: 2 client retries (immediate sanitize, then 1 re-call) + 1 repair attempt. Use exponential backoff on re-calls (500ms -> 1s). If you want zero client work, we can enable server-side repair for your org temporarily while we collect examples — let me know and I'll open an internal config request.

sara (Acme): please enable server-side repair while we roll this out, that would help. thanks! :pray:

maya (support): done — enabled `org:acme` server-side repair flag for 72h, will monitor. please DM me any further broken samples.

jon (eng): note to self: follow up to add a small dataset to infra/evals for malformed-json and measure improvements after repair is enabled.

sara (Acme): will send samples tonight. thanks everyone!
